# Releasing

Branch names carry a prefix, listed in [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md#branches).
`.github/workflows/label.yml` labels a new pull request from it, and
`.github/release.yml` sorts the release notes by label:

| Prefix               | Label           | Release notes section |
| -------------------- | --------------- | --------------------- |
| `feature/`           | `enhancement`   | Features              |
| `bugfix/`, `hotfix/` | `bug`           | Fixes                 |
| `docs/`              | `documentation` | Documentation         |
| `release/`           | `release`       | Left out              |
| Anything else        | None            | Other changes         |

## Cutting a Release

1. On a `release/` branch, bump `version` in {% if is_workspace %}`[workspace.package]`{% else %}`[package]`{% endif %} and merge it.
2. Tag the merge with the same version and push the tag:
   `git tag v0.2.0 && git push origin v0.2.0`. A tag that does not match the version
   fails the workflow before anything is built.
3. `.github/workflows/release.yml` drafts a GitHub release{% if has_bin %} with the executables for
   Linux (x86_64, aarch64), macOS (Apple Silicon, Intel), and Windows (x86_64), plus a
   `SHA256SUMS` list{% endif %}.
   Review the draft and publish it by hand.
{%- if has_bin %}

Running the workflow from the Actions tab builds the same executables into a workflow
artifact without making a release.

The executables are not signed. A downloaded one needs `chmod +x` on Linux and macOS,
and macOS quarantines it until `xattr -d com.apple.quarantine <file>`.
{%- endif %}
{%- if publish %}

## Publishing to crates.io

Before the first `cargo publish`, fill in `keywords` and `categories` in{% if is_workspace %}
`[workspace.package]`. Publish `{{project-name}}-core` before `{{project-name}}`, which depends
on it by version.{% else %} `[package]`.{% endif %}
{%- endif %}
